            "The <strong>shake test</strong> detects freeze damage in the adsorbed vaccines (pentavalent, Td, hepatitis B, PCV where validated): take a vial of the same vaccine, batch and manufacturer that has deliberately been frozen solid and thawed as a control; shake the suspect vial and the control together and stand them side by side; the frozen-and-thawed control sediments quickly with a clear supernatant and granular sediment; if the suspect vial behaves the same — fast sedimentation, clear liquid above — it has been frozen and <strong>the whole batch exposed to the same conditions is discarded</strong>; if it stays uniformly cloudy longer than the control it is usable. <strong>Conditioning ice packs</strong> is the daily habit that prevents the problem: frozen packs are left at room temperature until they 'sweat' and <strong>the ice moves inside when shaken</strong> (water is audible), which means their surface is at about 0 °C rather than −20; only then are they packed around the vaccines in the carrier, with the vaccines in the centre and the foam pad on top, and the carrier closed and kept in the shade. Freeze-sensitive vaccines never touch an ice pack; some programmes wrap them in paper or use cool-water packs for outreach.",
            "<strong>When the power fails or the fridge is found warm</strong>: do not open the door; note the time; check the temperature record and alarm history to see how long and how high; move vaccines to a working fridge or a packed cold box if the outage will be long; read the VVMs; do the shake test on freeze-sensitive vaccines if the temperature went below 0; <strong>quarantine</strong> the vaccines with a 'do not use' label and report to the district cold-chain officer, who decides what is discarded. Never quietly discard and never quietly use.",
