            "An <strong>adverse event following immunisation (AEFI)</strong> is any untoward medical occurrence after immunisation, <strong>whether or not the vaccine caused it</strong>. The definition is deliberately wide: a child who dies of malaria two days after her measles vaccine is an AEFI until the investigation says otherwise, because the mother and the community will connect the two, and a programme that dismisses her concern loses the community. The WHO classifies AEFIs by cause: <strong>vaccine product-related</strong> (the known reactions — local soreness, fever, the BCG scar, rarely anaphylaxis); <strong>vaccine quality defect</strong> (a faulty batch — very rare); <strong>immunisation error</strong> ('programme error' — wrong diluent, non-sterile technique, wrong site, frozen vaccine — the commonest cause of serious harm and entirely preventable); <strong>immunisation anxiety</strong> (fainting, hyperventilation and 'mass psychogenic illness', typical of adolescent campaigns such as HPV, where a cluster of girls faint in turn); and <strong>coincidental</strong> (illness that would have happened anyway — in a country where infants have a fever every few weeks, the days after vaccination will contain some of them).",
            "<strong>Minor expected reactions</strong> are common and self-limiting: local pain, redness and swelling in up to one in ten; fever under 39 °C, irritability and poor appetite for a day or two after pentavalent and PCV; a mild rash and fever five to twelve days after measles–rubella in about one in twenty; the BCG papule, small ulcer and scar. They are managed with explanation in advance, breastfeeding, paracetamol if the child is distressed and a cool compress, and they are not reasons to withhold the next dose.",
