            "<strong>Coverage</strong> for an antigen is the number of doses given to the target group in a period divided by the target population, times 100: if 1,000 infants under one year live in a sub-district and 880 received pentavalent 3 this year, Penta 3 coverage is 88 per cent. The target population comes from the census projection (infants under one are about 3.5–4 per cent of the population) corrected by the sub-district head count in the <strong>microplan</strong>, and a wrong denominator produces impossible coverage figures over 100 per cent — which is a signal to recount, not to celebrate. <strong>Penta 1 coverage measures access</strong> — did the children reach the service at all; <strong>Penta 3 and MR coverage measure utilisation</strong> — did they come back; the <strong>dropout rate</strong> measures the gap: <strong>(Penta 1 − Penta 3) ÷ Penta 1 × 100</strong>, and the same between Penta 1 and MR 1, and between MR 1 and MR 2. A dropout rate <strong>above 10 per cent</strong> is a problem of utilisation — distance, waiting times, rude staff, a reaction after the first dose that nobody explained, no reminder of the next date, a mother who went to the farm — and the solution is in the service; <strong>low Penta 1 with low dropout</strong> is a problem of access — a community the outreach does not reach — and the solution is more or better-placed sessions. The <strong>monitoring chart</strong> on the facility wall plots cumulative doses against the monthly target line so that a sub-district falling behind is seen in March, not in December.",
